1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:10,290
This video is about independent records: how WildObserve counts each visit by an animal once, where you'll find those counts, and how to export them.

2
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:16,700
A camera trap takes a burst of pictures each time an animal passes.

3
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:27,120
An animal that stays in front of the camera, or comes back a few minutes later, fills many pictures, so counting just pictures would count this one deer many times.

4
00:00:28,290 --> 00:00:33,110
That's why camera-trap studies count independent records instead.

5
00:00:33,110 --> 00:00:34,510
You'll find them in the Dashboard.

6
00:00:35,957 --> 00:00:47,157
For each species, the Species table shows its images, its capture events, which are bursts of pictures taken together, and its independent records.

7
00:00:47,157 --> 00:00:51,517
Here, hundreds of pictures of mule deer are from only a handful of visits.

8
00:00:52,311 --> 00:00:59,011
A species' sightings at one camera count as one record while they're within the same interval.

9
00:00:59,011 --> 00:01:03,651
In WildObserve, 30 minutes is the default interval.

10
00:01:03,651 --> 00:01:14,311
Each sighting is compared with the one before it, so an animal that stays for an hour is still one visit, and a new record starts once it's been gone for 30 minutes.

11
00:01:15,481 --> 00:01:19,001
Studies use different intervals, and you can change it.

12
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:22,221
Close the Dashboard and open Deployments.

13
00:01:22,221 --> 00:01:26,361
The interval is near the top, under the gap for capture events.

14
00:01:26,361 --> 00:01:29,941
Type the new interval, here 10 minutes, and click Save.

15
00:01:31,111 --> 00:01:35,891
Records aren't stored, so they're counted again right away.

16
00:01:35,891 --> 00:01:41,311
Back in the Dashboard, rabbits, which come and go, now have more records than before.

17
00:01:42,481 --> 00:01:49,061
To use the records in R or a spreadsheet, close the Dashboard and click Export.

18
00:01:49,061 --> 00:01:51,821
Choose CSV spreadsheet.

19
00:01:51,821 --> 00:01:55,481
The screen says which interval the records use.

20
00:01:55,481 --> 00:01:57,241
Then click Export to save the file.

21
00:01:58,411 --> 00:02:01,051
The file has one row per box.

22
00:02:01,051 --> 00:02:09,211
Its last columns give each image's deployment and when it was taken, and each box's independent record.

23
00:02:09,211 --> 00:02:17,171
Every box from one visit shares a number, so to count a species' records at a camera, count its different records.

24
00:02:18,341 --> 00:02:21,601
The other export formats are in the same list.

25
00:02:21,601 --> 00:02:27,161
You can find more videos, and the user guide, at QSC.earth/WildObserve.
